# Topic: a host's readings around the Luau calls it samples
#
# What it does: lua_setprofilecalls and lua_setprofilecallevery. While a profile's call hook is set, each Luau call's
# frame set-up (an interpreted CALL, a call from C, a fast pcall, a metamethod's fast path) asks whether to sample it:
# each call of a function is drawn on its own, with a chance of one in `every` (the host sets `every` per function; 1
# until set; 0 for none, and then one call after every `remeasure` is read around for its length alone), except that a
# call inside a sampled call of its own function on the same thread is sampled too (up to 64 from each recursion's
# outermost sampled call), so a recursion's estimate does not grow noisier with each level. A sampled call's frame is
# flagged (LUA_CALLINFO_PROFILECALL) and the hook runs as its first instruction is about to run and as its RETURN runs,
# or as an error unwinds it to a protected call or to a coroutine's handler, or as an error ends its thread or the
# thread is reset, the frame in place each time, so the host can read the CPU's counters around exactly the code the
# call ran. Each thread's open sampled calls are tracked, so a thread that goes (a freed one too) gives them up. Calls
# into native code are not sampled. Off, the interpreter does not ask: luau_execute runs a copy of its own that asks
# only while a call hook is set (global_State::profilecalls, in padding: the state keeps its size), as it runs one for
# single-stepping, compiled in a translation unit of its own (lvmexecute_profiled.cpp) so that both copies build side
# by side; a call from C or a metamethod's fast path loads that flag, and a return tests a frame flag it loads anyway.
#
# Why upstream lacks it: Luau's hooks are per instruction (single-step) or at safepoints, neither of which marks a
# call's entry and return; a hardware-counter profile needs both, and cheaply enough to skip most calls.

diff --git a/luau/VM/include/lua.h b/luau/VM/include/lua.h
